+  bigIdea:
+    "Political power became divided among several Muslim states, while religion, commerce, and scholarship kept people connected across borders. A shared cultural world could survive the decline of a particular empire.",
+  context:
+    "Islam began in seventh-century Arabia, long before this unit. By about 1200, Abbasid caliphs in Baghdad had already lost much of their political control. Focus on what changed between 1200 and 1450, using earlier developments to explain how the region reached that point.",
+  sections: [
+    {
+      id: "islam-states",
+      title: "One religious world, several governments",
+      conceptTitle: "States and political change",
+      lenses: [],
+      blocks: [
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Dar al-Islam refers here to the lands of Muslim rule and the wider Islamic cultural world. It was not the name of one country. As Abbasid authority weakened, regional rulers and military elites gained power. Many came from Turkic backgrounds, but the populations they governed remained diverse.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "The Seljuks illustrate this shift beginning in the eleventh century. Their rulers used the title sultan and exercised military and political authority, while the Abbasid caliph retained religious prestige. The great Seljuk empire had already broken up before 1200; related successor states, including the Sultanate of Rum in Anatolia, continued into this period.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "In Egypt, Mamluk military leaders took power in 1250. Mamluks were soldiers originally recruited through enslavement; their rise shows how a military elite could become a ruling class. Their sultanate governed Egypt and Syria and benefited from commerce linking the Mediterranean and Red Sea. The Delhi Sultanate, founded in 1206, established another center of Muslim power in northern India, where most subjects did not become Muslim.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Mongol forces captured Baghdad in 1258, ending Abbasid rule there. This was a major political rupture, but it did not end Islam or scholarship throughout the region. Mamluk forces checked Mongol expansion at Ayn Jalut in 1260. Christian crusading states also formed part of the region’s political conflicts; no single war explains every change.",
