+      conceptTitle: "Religion and society",
+      lenses: [],
+      blocks: [
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Islam is monotheistic: Muslims worship one God and regard Muhammad as a prophet. The Quran is its sacred scripture. Practices commonly summarized as the Five Pillars include a declaration of faith, prayer, charity, fasting during Ramadan, and pilgrimage to Mecca for those able to make it. These practices connected local communities with a wider religious community.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Islamic legal traditions, commonly discussed under the term sharia, shaped matters such as inheritance, family obligations, and commerce. Scholars interpreted religious sources; practice varied across places and schools of law. Sharing a religious framework did not mean every ruler applied identical rules.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Jewish and Christian communities also maintained their beliefs, worship, and communal institutions. Under many Muslim rulers they could practice their religions but held a subordinate legal position, with special taxes or other restrictions. Cooperation in trade and scholarship coexisted with discrimination and episodes of persecution. Avoid describing every society as either completely tolerant or uniformly oppressive.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Social life remained hierarchical. Women could have property and inheritance rights under Islamic law, yet patriarchal customs and unequal legal treatment limited opportunities. Class, location, and family circumstances mattered. Legal rights are evidence about institutions; they do not prove that everyone experienced equal freedom.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Judaism emphasized one God, covenant, and religious law, with communal learning and worship supporting Jewish identity across dispersed communities. Christianity centered on Jesus, salvation, scripture, and church communities. Sunni and Shi’a traditions reflected different views of legitimate leadership and religious authority within Islam. These differences mattered, but did not prevent all cooperation across religious boundaries.",
+        },
+      ],
+      takeaway:
+        "Connect a belief or practice to its effect: charity to communal obligations, pilgrimage to wider contact, or religious law to social relationships.",
+    },
+    {
+      id: "islam-spread",
+      title: "Trade carried more than goods",
+      conceptTitle: "Trade and the spread of Islam",
+      lenses: [],
+      blocks: [
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Separate the expansion of Muslim rule from the spread of Islam as a faith. Armies could establish a Muslim government without converting the whole population. Merchants, teachers, and religious communities could introduce Islam where no Muslim army had conquered territory.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Muslim merchants traveled through Indian Ocean ports, Central Asian markets, and African trading networks. Repeated contact created opportunities to share practices and form communities. Commercial relationships and religious connections could reinforce one another, although conversion was gradual and had different causes in different places.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Sufis emphasized spiritual discipline and a close relationship with God. Teachers and their communities helped communicate Islam through personal instruction, devotion, and sometimes local languages or customs. Their approaches varied; Sufism was part of Islamic life, not simply a separate religion opposed to learning.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Trade also supported urban wealth and patronage. Cairo connected regional and long-distance commerce, while pilgrimage and travel brought people from different societies together. These networks help explain why a change in political borders did not erase cultural exchange.",
+        },
+      ],
+      takeaway:
+        "Military expansion, trade, and religious teaching were distinct processes that could overlap. Explain the mechanism shown by your evidence.",
+    },
+    {
+      id: "islam-learning",
+      title: "Scholars developed knowledge as well as preserving it",
+      conceptTitle: "Learning and innovation",
+      lenses: [],
+      blocks: [
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Earlier Abbasid rulers supported translation and scholarship in Baghdad. The House of Wisdom is associated with this earlier intellectual setting. Scholars worked with Greek philosophy, mathematical knowledge from India, and techniques such as papermaking that had spread from China. Translation made knowledge available to new readers; commentary could challenge or extend it.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Nasir al-Din al-Tusi, who lived from 1201 to 1274, contributed to mathematics and astronomy and directed work at the Maragha observatory in present-day Iran. His work provides an example of continued intellectual activity during the era of Mongol rule, rather than proof that all scholarship stopped after 1258.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Medical learning also developed through texts, hospitals, observation, and teaching. Scholars did more than copy older works: they compared explanations and added new findings. Ibn Khaldun, who lived from 1332 to 1406, studied how social organization and political power changed over time, showing that intellectual inquiry included human societies.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "The course framework also names the Sufi writer A’ishah al-Ba’uniyyah as a literary example. She lived after 1450, so use her as evidence of the continuation of this literary tradition beyond the unit’s main period, not as someone writing around 1200.",
+        },
+      ],
+      takeaway:
+        "For an innovation, explain what scholars added and why it mattered. For an inherited idea, explain how it was preserved, interpreted, or used.",
+    },
+    {
+      id: "islam-exchange",
+      title: "Ideas moved across languages and religions",
+      conceptTitle: "Intellectual exchange",
+      lenses: [],
+      blocks: [
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Al-Andalus, the Muslim-ruled parts of the Iberian Peninsula, was one important setting for intellectual contact. Scholars writing in Arabic included Muslims, Jews, and Christians. Political boundaries changed, but translation and study also continued in Christian-ruled centers such as Toledo.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Works of Greek philosophy, along with Arabic commentaries and scientific writings, reached Latin-reading audiences through translation. The earlier philosopher Ibn Rushd, known in Latin as Averroes, wrote influential commentaries on Aristotle. Jewish thinkers such as Maimonides also participated in this wider intellectual world.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "This exchange expanded the materials available to scholars in Europe and elsewhere. It was a process involving many societies, languages, and generations. Saying that one civilization invented all knowledge, or that another merely copied it, misses both the transfers and the original contributions.",
+        },
+        {
+          type: "paragraph",
+          text: "Compare this with East Asia: people in both regions adopted and reworked ideas from elsewhere. Chinese influence in Korea and Japan did not require one government over all three societies; similarly, shared Islamic scholarship did not require a single caliph to control every city.",
