-// Original study material. References are listed with the lesson; source PDFs are not distributed.
-const topicId='world-1-2';
-export const topic12Concepts={
- 'islam-states':{title:'States and political change',section:'islam-states'},
- 'islam-beliefs':{title:'Religion and society',section:'islam-beliefs'},
- 'islam-spread':{title:'Trade and the spread of Islam',section:'islam-spread'},
- 'islam-learning':{title:'Learning and innovation',section:'islam-learning'},
- 'islam-exchange':{title:'Intellectual exchange',section:'islam-exchange'}
-};
-export const topic12Lesson={
- minutes:10,
- bigIdea:'Political power became divided among several Muslim states, while religion, commerce, and scholarship kept people connected across borders. A shared cultural world could survive the decline of a particular empire.',
- context:'Islam began in seventh-century Arabia, long before this unit. By about 1200, Abbasid caliphs in Baghdad had already lost much of their political control. Focus on what changed between 1200 and 1450, using earlier developments to explain how the region reached that point.',
- sections:[
-  {id:'islam-states',title:'One religious world, several governments',paragraphs:[
-   'Dar al-Islam refers here to the lands of Muslim rule and the wider Islamic cultural world. It was not the name of one country. As Abbasid authority weakened, regional rulers and military elites gained power. Many came from Turkic backgrounds, but the populations they governed remained diverse.',
-   'The Seljuks illustrate this shift beginning in the eleventh century. Their rulers used the title sultan and exercised military and political authority, while the Abbasid caliph retained religious prestige. The great Seljuk empire had already broken up before 1200; related successor states, including the Sultanate of Rum in Anatolia, continued into this period.',
-   'In Egypt, Mamluk military leaders took power in 1250. Mamluks were soldiers originally recruited through enslavement; their rise shows how a military elite could become a ruling class. Their sultanate governed Egypt and Syria and benefited from commerce linking the Mediterranean and Red Sea. The Delhi Sultanate, founded in 1206, established another center of Muslim power in northern India, where most subjects did not become Muslim.',
-   'Mongol forces captured Baghdad in 1258, ending Abbasid rule there. This was a major political rupture, but it did not end Islam or scholarship throughout the region. Mamluk forces checked Mongol expansion at Ayn Jalut in 1260. Christian crusading states also formed part of the region’s political conflicts; no single war explains every change.'
-  ],takeaway:'Name the change precisely: political control shifted to regional states. Religious and cultural connections continued across those states.'},
-  {id:'islam-beliefs',title:'Belief shaped daily life and social rules',paragraphs:[
-   'Islam is monotheistic: Muslims worship one God and regard Muhammad as a prophet. The Quran is its sacred scripture. Practices commonly summarized as the Five Pillars include a declaration of faith, prayer, charity, fasting during Ramadan, and pilgrimage to Mecca for those able to make it. These practices connected local communities with a wider religious community.',
-   'Islamic legal traditions, commonly discussed under the term sharia, shaped matters such as inheritance, family obligations, and commerce. Scholars interpreted religious sources; practice varied across places and schools of law. Sharing a religious framework did not mean every ruler applied identical rules.',
-   'Jewish and Christian communities also maintained their beliefs, worship, and communal institutions. Under many Muslim rulers they could practice their religions but held a subordinate legal position, with special taxes or other restrictions. Cooperation in trade and scholarship coexisted with discrimination and episodes of persecution. Avoid describing every society as either completely tolerant or uniformly oppressive.',
-   'Social life remained hierarchical. Women could have property and inheritance rights under Islamic law, yet patriarchal customs and unequal legal treatment limited opportunities. Class, location, and family circumstances mattered. Legal rights are evidence about institutions; they do not prove that everyone experienced equal freedom.'
-  ],takeaway:'Connect a belief or practice to its effect: charity to communal obligations, pilgrimage to wider contact, or religious law to social relationships.'},
-  {id:'islam-spread',title:'Trade carried more than goods',paragraphs:[
-   'Separate the expansion of Muslim rule from the spread of Islam as a faith. Armies could establish a Muslim government without converting the whole population. Merchants, teachers, and religious communities could introduce Islam where no Muslim army had conquered territory.',
-   'Muslim merchants traveled through Indian Ocean ports, Central Asian markets, and African trading networks. Repeated contact created opportunities to share practices and form communities. Commercial relationships and religious connections could reinforce one another, although conversion was gradual and had different causes in different places.',
-   'Sufis emphasized spiritual discipline and a close relationship with God. Teachers and their communities helped communicate Islam through personal instruction, devotion, and sometimes local languages or customs. Their approaches varied; Sufism was part of Islamic life, not simply a separate religion opposed to learning.',
-   'Trade also supported urban wealth and patronage. Cairo connected regional and long-distance commerce, while pilgrimage and travel brought people from different societies together. These networks help explain why a change in political borders did not erase cultural exchange.'
-  ],takeaway:'Military expansion, trade, and religious teaching were distinct processes that could overlap. Explain the mechanism shown by your evidence.'},
-  {id:'islam-learning',title:'Scholars developed knowledge as well as preserving it',paragraphs:[
-   'Earlier Abbasid rulers supported translation and scholarship in Baghdad. The House of Wisdom is associated with this earlier intellectual setting. Scholars worked with Greek philosophy, mathematical knowledge from India, and techniques such as papermaking that had spread from China. Translation made knowledge available to new readers; commentary could challenge or extend it.',
-   'Nasir al-Din al-Tusi, who lived from 1201 to 1274, contributed to mathematics and astronomy and directed work at the Maragha observatory in present-day Iran. His work provides an example of continued intellectual activity during the era of Mongol rule, rather than proof that all scholarship stopped after 1258.',
-   'Medical learning also developed through texts, hospitals, observation, and teaching. Scholars did more than copy older works: they compared explanations and added new findings. Ibn Khaldun, who lived from 1332 to 1406, studied how social organization and political power changed over time, showing that intellectual inquiry included human societies.',
-   'The course framework also names the Sufi writer A’ishah al-Ba’uniyyah as a literary example. She lived after 1450, so use her as evidence of the continuation of this literary tradition beyond the unit’s main period, not as someone writing around 1200.'
-  ],takeaway:'For an innovation, explain what scholars added and why it mattered. For an inherited idea, explain how it was preserved, interpreted, or used.'},
-  {id:'islam-exchange',title:'Ideas moved across languages and religions',paragraphs:[
-   'Al-Andalus, the Muslim-ruled parts of the Iberian Peninsula, was one important setting for intellectual contact. Scholars writing in Arabic included Muslims, Jews, and Christians. Political boundaries changed, but translation and study also continued in Christian-ruled centers such as Toledo.',
-   'Works of Greek philosophy, along with Arabic commentaries and scientific writings, reached Latin-reading audiences through translation. The earlier philosopher Ibn Rushd, known in Latin as Averroes, wrote influential commentaries on Aristotle. Jewish thinkers such as Maimonides also participated in this wider intellectual world.',
-   'This exchange expanded the materials available to scholars in Europe and elsewhere. It was a process involving many societies, languages, and generations. Saying that one civilization invented all knowledge, or that another merely copied it, misses both the transfers and the original contributions.',
-   'Compare this with East Asia: people in both regions adopted and reworked ideas from elsewhere. Chinese influence in Korea and Japan did not require one government over all three societies; similarly, shared Islamic scholarship did not require a single caliph to control every city.'
-  ],takeaway:'Build an explanation with three parts: an idea, a route or group carrying it, and an effect on the receiving society.'}
- ],
- sources:[
-  {label:'AMSCO, World History Modern: AP Edition, Topic 1.2, printed pages 15–19 (class reading reference).'},
-  {label:'College Board: 2026 AP World History framework, Topic 1.2, printed pages 40–41',url:'https://apcentral.collegeboard.org/media/pdf/ap-world-history-modern-course-and-exam-description-effective-fall-2026.pdf'}
- ]
-};
-export const topic12Vocabulary=[
- ['dar','Dar al-Islam','The lands of Muslim rule and, in this course, the connected Islamic cultural world; not one unified state.'],
- ['abbasid','Abbasid Caliphate','A dynasty centered on Baghdad whose political authority fragmented; Mongol conquest ended its rule in Baghdad in 1258.'],
- ['caliph','Caliph','A leader claiming succession to Muhammad’s leadership of the Muslim community; actual political power varied over time.'],
- ['sultan','Sultan','A title used by Muslim rulers with political and military authority, sometimes alongside a caliph with religious prestige.'],
- ['seljuk','Seljuk Turks','Turkic Muslim rulers who gained power across much of the Middle East before 1200; successor states continued into the unit’s period.'],
- ['mamluk','Mamluk Sultanate','The state established in Egypt in 1250 by a military elite originally recruited through enslavement; it also governed Syria.'],
- ['delhi','Delhi Sultanate','A succession of Muslim-ruled dynasties based in Delhi from 1206; an example of Islamic state formation in South Asia.'],
- ['sharia','Sharia','Islamic religious and ethical law, interpreted through diverse scholarly traditions and applied differently across societies.'],
- ['sufi','Sufism','Islamic traditions emphasizing spiritual discipline and closeness to God; Sufi teachers helped spread the faith.'],
- ['pillars','Five Pillars','Central Islamic practices involving faith, prayer, charity, fasting, and pilgrimage for those able to undertake it.'],
- ['wisdom','House of Wisdom','An institution associated with Abbasid Baghdad’s earlier support for books, translation, and scholarship.'],
- ['tusi','Nasir al-Din al-Tusi','A thirteenth-century scholar known for work in mathematics and astronomy, including the Maragha observatory.'],
- ['andalus','Al-Andalus','The Muslim-ruled territories of the Iberian Peninsula, an important setting for exchange among different religious and intellectual traditions.'],
- ['transfer','Intellectual transfer','The movement of knowledge between communities, often through translation, travel, teaching, and commentary.']
-].map(([id,term,definition])=>({id:`islam-${id}`,topicId,term,definition}));
-export const topic12Connections=[
- {id:'islam-fragment',topicId,type:'Continuity / change',title:'A dynasty could fall while a cultural network endured.',body:'The capture of Baghdad changed political authority. Religious communities, trade, and scholarship continued in other centers. Distinguish the fate of one capital from developments across the entire Islamic world.'},
- {id:'islam-trade',topicId,type:'Cause → effect',title:'Repeated contact made religious exchange possible.',body:'Merchants and Sufi teachers built relationships across trading networks. Those relationships could introduce beliefs and practices without a conquering army, though contact did not guarantee conversion.'},
- {id:'islam-translation',topicId,type:'Process → effect',title:'Translation changed who could use an idea.',body:'Moving a work from Greek to Arabic or Arabic to Latin expanded its potential readership. Scholars could then debate and build on it. Translation, commentary, and new discoveries are related but distinct contributions.'},
- {id:'islam-song',topicId,type:'Comparison',title:'Compare sources of authority, not just rulers’ names.',body:'Song officials drew legitimacy from Confucian education and imperial administration. Muslim rulers could draw on Islamic law and religious patronage while military elites exercised political power. Both linked authority with cultural traditions, through different institutions.'},
- {id:'islam-diversity',topicId,type:'Evidence and limits',title:'Cooperation did not eliminate inequality.',body:'Shared intellectual work among Muslims, Jews, and Christians shows interaction. It does not establish equal legal status or prove that every community lived peacefully. A strong explanation recognizes both cooperation and restrictions.'}
-];
-export const topic12Questions=[
- {id:'islam-q1',concept:'islam-states',difficulty:'Core',skillTag:'Continuity and change',prompt:'Which statement best describes Dar al-Islam around 1200–1450?',choices:['One caliph directly governed every Muslim community.','Muslim political authority divided among states while religious and cultural links persisted.','The fall of one dynasty ended Islam throughout Afro-Eurasia.','Regional rulers abandoned all earlier Islamic institutions.'],correctAnswer:1,explanation:'New states held political power, yet religious practices, legal traditions, commerce, and learning connected communities across their borders. Dar al-Islam was not a single empire.'},
- {id:'islam-q2',concept:'islam-states',difficulty:'Apply',skillTag:'Using evidence',stimulus:'A military ruler commands an army and controls taxation while recognizing the religious prestige of a caliph in Baghdad.',prompt:'Which development does this situation best illustrate?',choices:['The disappearance of Muslim rule from the Middle East','Government by elected merchant assemblies','An equal sharing of authority among every religious community','A distinction between a sultan’s political power and a caliph’s religious prestige'],correctAnswer:3,explanation:'Seljuk rulers illustrate how sultans could hold practical military and political authority while Abbasid caliphs retained religious status. This is a change in the organization of power, not the end of Muslim government.'},
- {id:'islam-q3',concept:'islam-beliefs',difficulty:'Core',skillTag:'Explaining significance',prompt:'How could the pilgrimage to Mecca help connect Muslim societies?',choices:['It brought believers from different regions into contact through a shared religious practice.','It required all participating states to merge their governments.','It removed all differences in language and local custom.','It replaced commercial travel between cities.'],correctAnswer:0,explanation:'Pilgrimage joined a shared religious obligation with travel and encounters across regions. Shared practice did not erase cultural differences or create a unified government.'},
- {id:'islam-q4',concept:'islam-beliefs',difficulty:'Apply',skillTag:'Evidence and limits',stimulus:'Records from a Muslim-ruled city show Jewish and Christian communities maintaining worship and participating in trade, while facing special taxes and legal restrictions.',prompt:'Which conclusion accounts for all the evidence?',choices:['Religious communities had identical legal rights.','Religious diversity made economic cooperation impossible.','Religious coexistence could operate alongside unequal legal status.','Every resident had converted to Islam.'],correctAnswer:2,explanation:'The evidence includes both continuing communal life and restrictions. It supports neither full equality nor the claim that all coexistence was impossible. Conditions varied by place and ruler.'},
- {id:'islam-q5',concept:'islam-spread',difficulty:'Apply',skillTag:'Causation',stimulus:'A port community gradually adopts Islamic practices through contact with visiting merchants and a local Sufi teacher. No new army has taken control of the port.',prompt:'Which process best explains this change?',choices:['Political unification under the Abbasids','Religious diffusion through commerce and teaching','A compulsory conversion ordered by conquering soldiers','The end of long-distance exchange'],correctAnswer:1,explanation:'The scenario gives evidence of trade and religious teaching, not military conquest. These contacts could support conversion even without a new Muslim government.'},
- {id:'islam-q6',concept:'islam-spread',difficulty:'Apply',skillTag:'Historical reasoning',prompt:'Why should a historian distinguish the expansion of Muslim rule from the spread of Islam?',choices:['Muslim merchants never traveled outside Muslim-ruled states.','Political conquest always produced immediate religious uniformity.','Sufi teachers primarily collected imperial taxes.','A Muslim government could rule non-Muslims, and Islam could spread beyond its political borders.'],correctAnswer:3,explanation:'Political control and personal or communal religious change are different processes. The Delhi Sultanate ruled many non-Muslim subjects, while trading and teaching networks could carry Islam beyond state boundaries.'},
- {id:'islam-q7',concept:'islam-learning',difficulty:'Core',skillTag:'Explaining a development',prompt:'Which example most clearly illustrates intellectual innovation in the Islamic world?',choices:['Al-Tusi developing mathematical and astronomical work at an observatory','An army collecting taxes after occupying a city','A ruler replacing one governor with another','A caravan carrying an unchanged manuscript to a new town'],correctAnswer:0,explanation:'Al-Tusi’s mathematical and astronomical work added to knowledge. Carrying a manuscript is intellectual transfer; innovation specifically involves developing new methods, explanations, or findings.'},
- {id:'islam-q8',concept:'islam-learning',difficulty:'Apply',skillTag:'Using evidence',stimulus:'A student claims that the Mongol capture of Baghdad in 1258 ended intellectual activity everywhere in the Islamic world.',prompt:'Which evidence most directly challenges the claim?',choices:['Abbasid caliphs had supported scholars before 1200.','Baghdad was an important city before its conquest.','Al-Tusi conducted astronomical work under Mongol patronage, and Ibn Khaldun wrote in a later century.','Some Muslim rulers had Turkic backgrounds.'],correctAnswer:2,explanation:'The claim concerns all intellectual activity after 1258. Evidence of later scholarship directly contradicts it. Evidence only from before the conquest would not establish what happened afterward.'},
- {id:'islam-q9',concept:'islam-exchange',difficulty:'Apply',skillTag:'Explaining a process',prompt:'Which sequence best explains one route of intellectual exchange through Iberia?',choices:['Latin translation → the end of all Arabic scholarship → universal literacy','Military conquest → identical beliefs → no need for translation','Political division → total isolation → disappearance of written knowledge','Arabic texts and commentaries → translation into Latin → wider use by European scholars'],correctAnswer:3,explanation:'Translation allowed additional readers to study earlier works and Arabic commentaries. It expanded access and debate; it did not require religious uniformity or end scholarship in the source language.'},
- {id:'islam-q10',concept:'islam-exchange',difficulty:'Apply',skillTag:'Comparison',prompt:'What useful comparison links Islamic intellectual exchange with Chinese cultural influence in Korea and Japan?',choices:['Both involved receiving societies adopting and adapting ideas across political boundaries.','Both required every receiving society to become a province of one empire.','Both prevented local traditions from shaping borrowed ideas.','Both spread ideas only through military conquest.'],correctAnswer:0,explanation:'In both cases, cultural influence crossed political borders and involved local choices. Contact and adaptation help explain the similarities without assuming one government controlled every society.'},
- {id:'islam-k1',concept:'islam-states',difficulty:'Quick check',skillTag:'Concept check',prompt:'Which state emerged when a military elite took power in Egypt in 1250?',choices:['Song dynasty','Mamluk Sultanate','Delhi Sultanate','Abbasid Caliphate'],correctAnswer:1,explanation:'Mamluk leaders established their sultanate in Egypt in 1250. Delhi was a separate center of Muslim rule in northern India; the Song governed in China.'},
- {id:'islam-k2',concept:'islam-spread',difficulty:'Quick check',skillTag:'Concept check',prompt:'What role did Sufi teachers play in the spread of Islam?',choices:['They required all communities to abandon local languages.','They ended religious teaching outside Baghdad.','They introduced religious ideas through spiritual instruction and community relationships.','They united every Muslim state under a single sultan.'],correctAnswer:2,explanation:'Sufi teachers helped communicate Islam through spiritual practice and personal relationships. Their methods varied, and their influence did not depend on one unified state.'},
- {id:'islam-k3',concept:'islam-exchange',difficulty:'Quick check',skillTag:'Explaining significance',prompt:'Why did translating scholarly works into new languages matter?',choices:['It removed the need for anyone to study earlier ideas.','It proved all societies held identical beliefs.','It ensured every translated idea remained unchanged forever.','It allowed new groups of readers to study, debate, and develop the ideas.'],correctAnswer:3,explanation:'Translation widened access to knowledge. Readers could interpret or build on ideas, so intellectual transfer could also support further innovation.'}
