-  title:'South and Southeast Asia',
-  topic:[
-   ['asia-beliefs','Why did conversion to Islam not necessarily remove a person’s low social status in South Asia?','Local hierarchies also depended on occupation, wealth, and inherited relationships.',['Islam required every convert to enter the same caste.','All South Asian rulers immediately abolished occupational distinctions.','Conversion automatically supplied land and political office.'],'Religious affiliation could change while economic and social inequalities persisted. Beliefs about equality did not guarantee equal opportunities.','Continuity and change'],
-   ['asia-states','What best explains the coexistence of the Delhi Sultanate, Rajput kingdoms, and Vijayanagara?','Regional rulers maintained different political centers despite shared cultural connections.',['A single emperor appointed all three governments.','No regional state possessed any military organization.','Religious diversity prevented any ruler from collecting revenue.'],'South Asia had significant regional powers. Cultural links and Muslim expansion did not produce permanent political unity across the subcontinent.','Explaining a development'],
-   ['asia-devotion','Which comparison of Bhakti and Sufi traditions is most defensible?','Both could emphasize personal devotion and accessible religious teaching.',['Both were branches of Buddhism.','Both required all believers to reject local languages.','Both abolished every inherited social distinction.'],'Devotional teaching could appeal beyond elite circles. The traditions remained rooted in Hinduism and Islam, and neither erased social hierarchy everywhere.','Comparison'],
-   ['asia-sea','How could a ruler of a Southeast Asian port convert location into political power?','Attract shipping and collect revenue while maintaining commercial relationships.',['Replace all farming with compulsory study for Chinese examinations.','Prevent all foreign merchants from entering the region.','Eliminate competing ports simply by sharing their religion.'],'A strategic location created opportunities, but rulers needed institutions and relationships to turn commerce into resources.','Causation'],
-   ['asia-land','What do Khmer reservoirs and canals most directly help explain?','The agricultural base that sustained population and organized state activity.',['The absence of seasonal variation in rainfall.','The complete replacement of farming by imported food.','Direct Chinese administration of Angkor.'],'Water management helped communities handle seasonal conditions. Agricultural resources supported population and the labor available for political projects.','Using evidence'],
-   ['asia-land','A temple built for Vishnu later becomes a Buddhist place of worship. What does this change best illustrate?','Religious use could change while an important institution and site endured.',['Hinduism and Buddhism had always been identical.','A change of worship proves that every resident migrated.','The building could not have had any political significance.'],'Angkor Wat’s changing use illustrates both continuity of a sacred place and change in religious practice. It does not prove complete population replacement.','Continuity and change'],
-   ['asia-states','Why could temple patronage help Vijayanagara rulers maintain authority?','It associated rulers with sacred traditions and institutions controlling substantial resources.',['It removed the need for armies or land revenue.','It made all neighboring states acknowledge one emperor.','It guaranteed identical beliefs among all subjects.'],'Patronage connected political authority with respected religious institutions. It complemented rather than replaced material and military sources of power.','Causation'],
-   ['asia-devotion','Which evidence would best support a claim that Buddhism connected politically separate societies?','Monks traveled between Sri Lanka and mainland Southeast Asia to study and transmit religious practices.',['Every Buddhist community paid taxes to one ruler in India.','All maritime merchants became Buddhist government officials.','Theravada teachings originated independently in every port.'],'Travel and institutional relationships show cultural connections across political boundaries without requiring a single Buddhist state.','Using evidence']
-  ],quick:[
-   ['asia-devotion','What does Bhakti emphasize?','Devotion to a deity.',['Imperial examination recruitment.','Tribute paid only in gold.','A ban on local-language religious expression.'],'Bhakti traditions often expressed devotion through accessible poetry and teaching.'],
-   ['asia-sea','Which description of Majapahit is accurate?','A Javanese state with Hindu and Buddhist court traditions.',['A Christian kingdom in the Ethiopian highlands.','A northern Indian sultanate founded in 1206.','A Maya city-state ruled from Mayapan.'],'Majapahit arose on Java in 1293 and combined several cultural traditions with agrarian and maritime resources.'],
-   ['asia-land','Which pair connects a place to a relevant institution?','Sinhala kingdoms and Buddhist monastic patronage.',['Vijayanagara and Inca mit’a administration.','Angkor and European serfdom as an identical legal system.','Sukhothai and the Roman papacy.'],'Sri Lankan kingdoms supported Buddhist institutions, which also connected them with other regions.']
-  ]
- },
- 'world-1-4':{
-  title:'The Americas',
-  topic:[
-   ['americas-maya','Which statement best describes Maya political organization?','Competing city-states shared cultural traditions without one permanent unified government.',['All Maya cities were provinces of the Inca state.','A single elected council governed all Mesoamerica.','Maya political life ended completely before 1200.'],'Maya cities had their own rulers and relationships. Political changes did not erase Maya communities or traditions.','Explaining a development'],
-   ['americas-mexica','Why was retaining local rulers useful to the Mexica tributary system?','Local leaders could deliver required goods while imperial power enforced obligations.',['It prevented the collection of tribute.','It required every subject to move to the capital.','It turned all regional markets into independent empires.'],'Indirect administration could reduce the need to replace local institutions while still drawing resources from subject communities.','Causation'],
-   ['americas-mexica','What was the main relationship between chinampas and Tenochtitlan’s growth?','Intensive cultivation near the city helped supply a substantial urban population.',['Floating rafts moved all crops to the Andes.','The system made regional food production unnecessary.','Chinampas served only as temples without an agricultural function.'],'Constructed growing beds and channels increased productive land near the capital. Food supported urban life and specialization.','Causation'],
-   ['americas-inca','How did mit’a obligations strengthen Inca administration?','They supplied rotating labor for state projects and other needs.',['They selected officials through Confucian examinations.','They replaced every household with a paid military regiment.','They required European coins for all tax payments.'],'The state could mobilize labor for agriculture, infrastructure, and service. This was different from a wage contract or the later colonial mining system.','Explaining significance'],
-   ['americas-inca','What inference is supported by Inca roads, relay messengers, and khipus?','The state coordinated information and resources across difficult terrain.',['Every Inca resident read alphabetic books.','Trade required wheeled vehicles across the entire Andes.','Inca communities had no relationship with officials.'],'Communication and records supported administration. Complex government did not require an alphabetic script or wheeled transport.','Using evidence'],
-   ['americas-environment','Which comparison accurately connects farming to geography?','Chinampas used lake environments, while terraces helped manage Andean slopes.',['Both techniques depended on identical flat desert conditions.','Terraces were drifting gardens and chinampas were mountain roads.','Neither technique affected usable farmland.'],'Both supported cultivation, but each responded to different physical conditions. Similar goals did not require identical technology.','Comparison'],
-   ['americas-north','Why is European disease an unsuitable explanation for Mesa Verde’s thirteenth-century relocation?','The relocation preceded European arrival in that region by centuries.',['No American community ever experienced disease.','All environmental explanations are automatically correct.','Mesa Verde was a Spanish colonial town by 1200.'],'An explanation must fit the chronology. Environmental and social pressures are relevant; later European contact cannot cause an earlier event.','Historical reasoning'],
-   ['americas-north','What can Cahokia’s large earthen mounds most directly support?','The community organized substantial labor and resources.',['Every resident held the same political rank.','A written constitution identical to China’s governed the city.','European architects directed all construction.'],'Monuments provide evidence of coordinated work. More evidence is needed to reconstruct specific institutions and social relationships.','Evidence and limits']
-  ],quick:[
-   ['americas-mexica','What is tribute?','An obligation imposed on a subordinate community.',['A voluntary retail purchase.','A writing system based on knots.','A Buddhist monastic rule.'],'Tribute commonly took the form of goods supplied to a ruling power.'],
-   ['americas-inca','Which tool helped Inca officials keep records?','Khipus.',['Chinampas.','Fiefs.','Civil service examination essays.'],'Khipus used knotted cords to record information useful to administration.'],
-   ['americas-maya','What continued after the decline of many Classic Maya centers?','Maya communities, traditions, and later political centers.',['One permanent empire over all of the Americas.','Government by European monarchs before 1200.','The disappearance of all Maya languages.'],'Urban decline did not mean the end of Maya peoples.']
-  ]
- },
- 'world-1-5':{
-  title:'Africa',
-  topic:[
-   ['africa-kinship','Which comparison of Mali and the Hausa city-states is accurate?','Mali developed an empire, while Hausa cities retained separate rulers.',['Both had one shared ruler throughout 1200-1450.','Neither society participated in regional exchange.','Every Hausa city was governed by an Inca official.'],'These societies organized authority at different scales. Economic and cultural links did not require political unity.','Comparison'],
-   ['africa-mali','How did trans-Saharan exchange contribute to Mali’s political power?','Revenue from commerce helped sustain rulers, armies, and administration.',['Gold automatically eliminated disputes over succession.','Caravans replaced the need for agricultural producers.','Trade prevented religious and cultural connections.'],'Rulers could use commercial revenue as one source of resources. Agriculture and political organization remained necessary.','Causation'],
-   ['africa-mali','How could Mansa Musa’s pilgrimage support his authority?','It strengthened his visible Islamic identity and connections with influential religious networks.',['It made all Malian subjects immediately Muslim.','It transferred the Malian throne to the pope.','It ended all local traditions associated with kingship.'],'The pilgrimage connected sacred prestige with international relationships. It did not produce complete religious uniformity.','Explaining significance'],
-   ['africa-swahili','Which interpretation of Swahili cities best accounts for African and imported cultural elements?','Local communities actively shaped cities connected to overseas exchange.',['Foreign goods prove that Africans could not build urban centers.','Arabic vocabulary means Swahili is not a Bantu language.','Islamic influence meant all coastal cities had one government.'],'Connections can influence language and institutions without removing local agency or separate governments.','Evidence and limits'],
-   ['africa-zimbabwe','What would imported ceramics found at Great Zimbabwe most directly suggest?','Connections between an inland center and distant exchange networks.',['The site was located on the Mediterranean coast.','The buildings were necessarily constructed by foreign rulers.','Its population relied exclusively on imported food.'],'Imported objects support exchange connections, often through intermediaries. They do not establish who built or ruled the site.','Using evidence'],
-   ['africa-ethiopia','How do Lalibela’s churches challenge the claim that African state building relied exclusively on Islam?','They demonstrate Christian patronage linked with Ethiopian kingship.',['They prove religion never influenced any African state.','They show that all African rulers followed the Roman pope.','They date exclusively to European colonial rule.'],'Ethiopia provides a specific Christian example within Africa’s broader religious diversity.','Evaluating a claim'],
-   ['africa-culture','Why should a historian examine the context of a griot’s account?','Its audience and transmission can shape how rulers and events are remembered.',['Oral sources contain no usable historical information.','Written sources are always free of perspective.','Every performance was a transcript made by a court scribe.'],'Oral histories can preserve important evidence, but their context matters. The same principle applies to written and material sources.','Sourcing'],
-   ['africa-kinship','What is a problem with saying kin-based societies had no government?','It overlooks institutions that organized decisions and resolved disputes.',['It assumes they always had an elected national parliament.','It understates their universal use of Chinese examinations.','It overlooks their permanent political unity with Ethiopia.'],'Political institutions can operate through elders, chiefs, and lineages without a single centralized empire.','Historical reasoning']
-  ],quick:[
-   ['africa-zimbabwe','Where was Great Zimbabwe?','Inland southern Africa.',['Central Mexico.','The northern European coast.','The island of Java.'],'Its inland location makes connections through coastal intermediaries important.'],
-   ['africa-ethiopia','Which tradition was important to medieval Ethiopian kingship?','Christianity.',['Neo-Confucian examination learning.','Maya calendrical kingship.','The Bhakti movement as a state religion.'],'Christian institutions and claims to sacred ancestry supported rulers.'],
-   ['africa-swahili','What connected many Swahili cities to distant markets?','Indian Ocean exchange.',['The Grand Canal of China.','Inca roads across the Andes.','The English manorial court.'],'Coastal cities linked African producers with overseas merchants.']
-  ]
- },
- 'world-1-6':{
-  title:'Europe',
-  topic:[
-   ['europe-power','Why did many medieval European monarchs depend on local nobles?','Nobles controlled land, armed followers, and resources the monarch could not easily administer directly.',['Every noble passed a centrally administered Confucian examination.','No monarch claimed authority over territory.','Nobles lacked any connection to agricultural production.'],'Limits on revenue and direct administration made local power holders important. Monarchical authority varied across regions.','Causation'],
-   ['europe-manors','Which distinction between feudal relationships and manorialism is most useful?','Feudal ties linked elites through land and service; manorialism organized estate production and peasant obligations.',['Feudalism governed only trade at sea; manorialism governed only universities.','They were identical systems with no meaningful distinction.','Manorialism required all peasants to be free of obligations.'],'The two could reinforce one another while describing different relationships. Not every peasant had the same status.','Comparison'],
-   ['europe-faith','Which example shows the Church functioning as more than a place of worship?','A monastery manages land, supports a school, and influences regional patrons.',['A merchant uses an Andean terrace.','A ruler abolishes every religious institution in Europe.','A guild operates without any local community.'],'Religious institutions also held resources, promoted education, and participated in political relationships.','Using evidence'],
-   ['europe-faith','Which statement best describes religious life in medieval Europe?','Christianity was influential alongside Jewish and Muslim communities, with both exchange and persecution.',['Every person followed the same Christian church.','Religious difference prevented all translation or trade.','All religious communities enjoyed identical legal rights everywhere.'],'Europe included several religious traditions and unequal relationships. Neither uniformity nor universal equality fits the evidence.','Explaining a development'],
-   ['europe-agriculture','How could agricultural improvements contribute to town growth?','Additional food could support people working in crafts, trade, and other urban occupations.',['Every improvement immediately ended estate labor obligations.','Crop rotation eliminated the need for farmers.','Higher output made regional exchange unnecessary.'],'A surplus supported specialization, but technology worked alongside climate, land, and labor.','Causation'],
-   ['europe-agriculture','Which explanation best connects the Black Death to changing labor relations in parts of western Europe?','Fewer workers could increase labor’s bargaining power, while elites tried to preserve obligations.',['Population losses guaranteed higher rents for every lord.','The plague caused serfdom to end immediately everywhere.','Disease had no possible economic consequences.'],'Labor scarcity could change bargaining, but laws, coercion, and regional differences shaped outcomes.','Causation'],
-   ['europe-exchange','Why is the Fourth Crusade’s attack on Constantinople important for interpreting religious conflict?','Political and commercial interests could lead crusaders to attack other Christians.',['Every crusade was a peaceful trading expedition.','Constantinople was the capital of the Mexica state.','All Christian governments permanently merged after 1204.'],'The event complicates an explanation based only on opposing religious labels. Motives and alliances varied.','Historical reasoning'],
-   ['europe-learning','Which statement best describes early Renaissance humanism?','Renewed classical study and interest in human capacities could coexist with Christian belief.',['It required all scholars to abandon Christianity.','It eliminated differences in access to education.','It began with European conquest of the Inca before 1200.'],'Humanism altered intellectual emphasis without instantly replacing faith, institutions, or inequality.','Continuity and change']
-  ],quick:[
-   ['europe-manors','What commonly distinguished a serf from a free peasant?','Inherited obligations and restrictions tied to an estate.',['Guaranteed appointment as a royal official.','Exclusive rights to collect tribute from nobles.','Membership in the clergy.'],'Peasant statuses varied; serfdom involved specific constraints and obligations.'],
-   ['europe-power','What was a fief?','Land or rights granted in return for obligations.',['An Islamic pilgrimage.','A lake-based agricultural bed.','A knotted record.'],'Fiefs supported relationships between lords and vassals.'],
-   ['europe-learning','What does vernacular mean in literary history?','A locally used language.',['A universal language of every religion.','A special tax imposed on peasants.','An agricultural rotation system.'],'Vernacular writing used languages spoken by local communities rather than only a scholarly language such as Latin.']
-  ]
